{"paper_id": "pap_recursive_nanochat_autoresearch", "title": "First Steps Toward Automated AI Research", "authors": ["Recursive"], "year": 2026, "venue": {"name": "company blog", "peer_reviewed": false}, "urls": {"primary": "https://www.recursive.com/articles/first-steps-toward-automated-ai-research"}, "version": "", "retrieved_at": "2026-07-28", "status": "active", "tags": ["ngram", "value_path", "optimizer", "external_sota"], "notes": "Improved 0.9372->0.9109 val_bpb on a 5-min NanoChat-autoresearch frame. Largest reported lever: hashed bigram/trigram embeddings mixed into the attention VALUE path via learned gates. Also: Muon optimizer, squared-ReLU MLPs, weight-decay schedule changes, token-shifting and byte-level features (vanilla-Transformer companion run only). Re-fetched 2026-07-28 specifically checking for FlashAttention/FA4/kernel claims: none found in the retrievable text -- no named attention-kernel lever is reported for the nanochat-autoresearch result.", "fingerprint": "8d2a52b6f0bc970c"}
{"paper_id": "pap_deepseek_v4", "title": "DeepSeek-V4: Towards Highly Efficient Million-Token Context Intelligence", "authors": ["DeepSeek-AI"], "year": 2026, "venue": {"name": "arXiv", "peer_reviewed": false}, "urls": {"primary": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.19348"}, "version": "", "retrieved_at": "2026-07-28", "status": "active", "tags": ["architecture", "residual_stream", "attention", "moe", "optimizer"], "notes": "DeepSeekMoE + multi-token prediction retained. New: (1) Manifold-Constrained Hyper-Connections (mHC), a generalized/constrained multi-stream residual connection upgrading plain residual addition; (2) hybrid Compressed Sparse Attention (CSA, KV compression + DeepSeek Sparse Attention) and Heavily Compressed Attention (HCA, aggressive KV compression, dense attention) for long-context efficiency; (3) adopts the Muon optimizer for faster convergence/stability. Secondary-source summary only; not independently read primary-source in full this pass.", "fingerprint": "853dc015ee2e1222"}
{"paper_id": "pap_flashattention4", "title": "FlashAttention-4: Algorithm and Kernel Pipelining Co-Design for Asymmetric Hardware Scaling", "authors": ["Dao, T.", "et al."], "year": 2026, "venue": {"name": "arXiv/blog (Princeton, Together AI, Meta, NVIDIA, Colfax)", "peer_reviewed": false}, "urls": {"primary": "https://blog.ai.princeton.edu/2026/03/12/flashattention-4-algorithm-and-kernel-pipelining-co-design-for-asymmetric-hardware-scaling/"}, "version": "", "retrieved_at": "2026-07-28", "status": "active", "tags": ["attention", "kernel", "systems_kernel", "hardware_specific"], "notes": "Released 2026-03-05. Ground-up attention-kernel redesign co-designed specifically for NVIDIA Blackwell (B200): treats Blackwell's tensor-core-throughput-vs-memory-bandwidth asymmetry as a first-class design constraint, hiding non-matmul work behind tensor-core work via an asymmetric pipeline schedule. Reports up to 1605 TFLOPs/s on B200 (71% util), 1.1-1.3x vs cuDNN 9.13, 2.1-2.7x vs Triton; up to 3.6x vs FA2 at 32768 seq len. All reported numbers are B200-measured; the design's core rationale (Blackwell's specific tensor-core/HBM ratio) does not describe Hopper (H200), which has a different asymmetry profile and already has a mature FA3 kernel. No H200 measurement is reported by the source.", "fingerprint": "0a9d46e1d81c390e"}
{"paper_id": "pap_hyperconnections", "title": "Hyper-Connections", "authors": ["Zhu, D.", "Huang, H.", "Huang, Z.", "Zeng, Y.", "Mao, Y.", "Wu, B.", "Min, Q.", "Zhou, X."], "year": 2024, "venue": {"name": "ICLR 2025 / arXiv 2409.19606", "peer_reviewed": true}, "urls": {"primary": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.19606"}, "version": "", "retrieved_at": "2026-07-28", "status": "active", "tags": ["residual_stream", "architecture"], "notes": "Expands the residual stream to n parallel streams (n x C dim) with learned dynamic mixing across depth, addressing the gradient-vanishing vs representation-collapse seesaw of plain residual connections. Reports training instability at scale as an open failure mode, fixed by the follow-up mHC paper (pap_manifold_constrained_hyperconnections).", "fingerprint": "9e6d43a51ef35fea"}
{"paper_id": "pap_manifold_constrained_hyperconnections", "title": "mHC: Manifold-Constrained Hyper-Connections", "authors": ["Xie, Z.", "Wei, Y.", "Cao, H.", "Zhao, C.", "Deng, C.", "Li, J.", "Dai, D.", "Gao, H.", "Chang, J.", "Yu, K.", "Zhao, L.", "Zhou, S.", "Xu, Z.", "Zhang, Z.", "Zeng, W.", "Hu, S.", "Wang, Y.", "Yuan, J.", "Wang, L.", "Liang, W."], "year": 2025, "venue": {"name": "arXiv 2512.24880 (DeepSeek-AI)", "peer_reviewed": false}, "urls": {"primary": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.24880"}, "version": "", "retrieved_at": "2026-07-28", "status": "active", "tags": ["residual_stream", "architecture", "stability"], "notes": "Primary source (supersedes the earlier secondary-source pap_deepseek_v4 summary for this mechanism). Diagnoses plain Hyper-Connections as breaking the identity-mapping property of a residual connection, causing training instability, restricted scalability, and increased memory-access cost. Fixes this via a manifold projection (abstract-level fetch did not surface whether this is literally Birkhoff-polytope/Sinkhorn-Knopp doubly-stochastic, as an independent Medium explainer claimed) that restores identity mapping. Abstract-level fetch surfaced no quantitative loss/perplexity/benchmark numbers -- flagged as a limitation below, not assumed.", "fingerprint": "1e5606f2ab5e41d3"}
{"paper_id": "pap_ngram_aware_memorization_regularizer", "title": "Early Detection and Reduction of Memorisation for Domain Adaptation and Instruction Tuning", "authors": ["Slack, D. L.", "Al Moubayed, N."], "year": 2025, "venue": {"name": "Transactions of the Association for Computational Linguistics (TACL)", "peer_reviewed": true}, "urls": {"primary": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11372"}, "version": "", "retrieved_at": "2026-07-28", "status": "active", "tags": ["ngram", "memorization", "regularization", "data_efficiency"], "notes": "Peer-reviewed (TACL 2025). Studies memorization during LM fine-tuning (Pythia/Llama3/Mistral, 1.4B-70B) and finds memorization rises sharply in early epochs, often before validation perplexity/eval performance is optimized -- directly analogous to this campaign's own finding that the current 10-shard operating point sits near a ~2-epoch overfitting boundary (paper 011). Introduces an n-gram memorization score for early stopping AND an n-gram-aware loss regularizer, reporting up to 40% memorization reduction; exact regularizer formulation and the precise downstream-performance cost were not surfaced by an abstract-level fetch (flagged as a limitation, full-text not yet read).", "fingerprint": "420ec354c73505a2"}
{"paper_id": "pap_ngpt_hypersphere", "title": "nGPT: Normalized Transformer with Representation Learning on the Hypersphere", "authors": ["Loshchilov, I.", "et al. (NVIDIA)"], "year": 2024, "venue": {"name": "arXiv 2410.01131", "peer_reviewed": false}, "urls": {"primary": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.01131"}, "version": "", "retrieved_at": "2026-07-28", "status": "active", "tags": ["normalization", "architecture", "convergence_speed"], "notes": "All vectors (embeddings, MLP/attention matrices, hidden states) normalized to unit norm; token representations move on a hypersphere, each layer contributing an increment controlled by learnable per-layer 'eigen learning rates'. Reports a 4x-20x reduction in the number of training steps to reach equal quality, depending on sequence length, plus improved stability.", "fingerprint": "eb3b74d2a25e3c92"}
{"paper_id": "pap_metr_nanogpt_speedrun_2026", "title": "Evidence on AI R&D Progress from NanoGPT (research note)", "authors": ["METR"], "year": 2026, "venue": {"name": "METR research note", "peer_reviewed": false}, "urls": {"primary": "https://metr.org/notes/2026-04-21-ai-rd-nanogpt-progress/"}, "version": "", "retrieved_at": "2026-07-28", "status": "active", "tags": ["speedrun", "architecture", "optimizer", "external_sota"], "notes": "Survey of the NanoGPT speedrun: 77 records, 36 contributors, May 2024 - Mar 2026, cutting time-to-target from 45min to 1.43min (31x) on 8xH100. Early records imported frontier techniques (RoPE, ReLU^2, QK-norm, FlexAttention); later records increasingly invented new ones (33%). Named inventions: record 58 'Paired Head Attention' (novel attention, no clear precedent) and record 62 'Bigram Hash Embedding' (2017 hash embeddings combined with DeepSeek Engram ideas). Also notes operator-norm/LMO optimizers (Muon, Scion, Gluon) with scaling-law transfer.", "fingerprint": "e0812f3109d7d1dd"}
